# Testing

{{/* why: "lets not worry about things that might happen in 0.001 percent of
instances. assume normal usage and sudden edge cases can be ironed out in
the future" (learn, 2026-11-21). "why do we have so many tests? can we remove
90% of them and still have the same confidence ... i feel like we were
adding tests just to add them" (sentry-devex). */ -}}
The goal is confidence that the product works in normal use, with as few
tests as possible. A test earns its place only if it would catch a realistic
break in what a user sees, does, or pays for. Fewer, truer tests beat more
tests.

## Write these

{{/* why: "lets make sure our system and verification flows test it end to
end properly instead of me having to catch simple issues like this"
(skill-studio). "whats wrong with creating real issues and triggering high
priority alerts while we test? ... why not confirm everything end to end"
(didthis). */ -}}
1. **Each main user flow, end to end, against the real app**: the real build,
   the real UI or API, real storage. One test per flow, written the way a user
   does it. Prefer real services, in their test or sandbox mode when they
   have one, over mocks. A few cents per run is fine. Ask before a run costs
   more than about $1 or messages a real person.
2. **A rule that must always hold** (a round trip gives back the input, a
   second run changes nothing, a total is never negative): one property test
   with random inputs, instead of a few hand-picked examples.
3. **A bug a user actually hit**: one regression test at the level the user
   saw it, that fails without the fix.
4. **A change to the format of saved data**: one test that loads real old
   data with the new code.

## Do not write these

{{/* why: the four kinds the user's audit prompt deletes every time, plus the
rare-case rule. Review rounds kept adding tests for "synthetic edge cases
with no effect on real files" (skill-studio #551). "also why do we keep
skipped tests?" (2026-11-19). */ -}}
- **Rare cases.** See "What counts as rare" below. Fix them when they
  happen.
- Tests of copy, styling, or view or DOM structure; of a library or the
  framework; of a mock returning what you told it to; of code only tests
  call; or of behavior another test already covers at another level.
- Skipped tests. Make a test run or delete it. Never add retries to make a
  flaky test pass: fix the cause or delete it.
- Tests written to raise a coverage number. Do not add coverage gates.

## What counts as rare

{{/* why: the user, 2026-11-21: "how do we define rare cases? i dont want it
to start missing things it should be catching". A frequency like "1 in
1,000" can't be measured by an agent, so the test is two questions about
normal use and cost, plus a list of common events that are never rare. */ -}}
A case is rare only when both answers are no:

1. Will a normal user hit it within a month of everyday use?
2. If it happens, does it lose or corrupt their data, cost them more than about a dollar,
   expose a secret, or leave them stuck with no way out?

These happen to every user, so they are never rare: no network or a slow
one, the app killed in the background, the phone restarting, a sign-in
that expires, the first launch with nothing saved, a new day, week or
month, headphones, Bluetooth or the car connecting and disconnecting, a
call interrupting, long or non-English text and emoji, and real data from
the real sources the app reads. Anything a user, a log or a crash report
has shown is never rare.

When you skip a case as rare, say so in your reply, in one line per case,
so the user can overrule you. Never skip one silently.

When a review finding is a rare case, answer "rare case, not fixed, per the
testing skill" and move on. Fix a rare case only when the fix is a line or
two and the reviewer showed how normal use reaches it, and then add no test.

## Test the real thing, quietly

{{/* why: "you keep using the fucking default narration from apple thats
absolutely garbage and it doesn't help" (learn, 2026-11-21): unit and UI
tests narrated with the compact system voice, out loud, while the product
uses a premium cloud voice. Earlier the same week UI tests played a video,
a podcast and speech through the Mac speakers and ran on the simulator that
held the user's logins. */ -}}
- Prove a feature with what the user gets, never a worse stand-in: not the
  default system voice for a premium voice, not a mock API for the real one,
  not a placeholder for real content. If the real thing costs money, record
  its real output once, check it in as a fixture, and replay it.
- Tests make no sound and open nothing on the user's screen. Mute every
  audio and video output in test runs.
- Never run tests on the user's own devices, simulators, browser profiles or
  accounts. Use separate ones.

## Before you hand back

{{/* why: "i want to test the final version that you're proud of not a half
baked implementation" (sentry-devex). The global rule "Do not say done
without a number or something you saw". */ -}}
- Run the changed flow for real and show the proof: a screenshot, a number,
  or the output. A green unit suite does not prove a feature works.
- Run the full suite. A failure is a bug or a bad test: fix it or delete the
  test. Never rerun until green.

## Briefing another agent

{{/* why: the junk tests in learn came from the orchestrator's own briefs:
"every finding gets a test that fails before the fix" and "find adversarial
edge cases" (2026-11-20). */ -}}
When you delegate code, a fix or a review, copy into the brief the first
paragraph of this skill and the list under "Do not write these". Never write
"every finding needs a test", and never ask a reviewer for adversarial edge
cases.

Other skills that ask for tests, such as TDD or bug diagnosis skills, decide
how to write a test. This skill decides whether a test is worth writing.
